      "id": "A01",
      "label": "order dates stored at 23:59:59.999 on June 30: SQL Server's datetime rounds them to July 1, which starts the next fiscal year",
      "traps": [
        "dates-datetime"
      ],
      "reasoning": "OrderDate is datetime: '2003-06-30T23:59:59.999' is stored as 2003-07-01 00:00:00.000 (1/300 s ticks), so DATEADD(m, 6, ...) gives 2004-01-01 and the amount lands in [2004]. The PostgreSQL column is timestamp(3) and keeps .999, so + 6 months is 2003-12-30 23:59:59.999 and the amount lands in [2003]. Person 2's '2002-06-30T23:59:59.998' rounds down to .997 on SQL Server and stays in fiscal 2002 on both.",
